Exact low-level rules behind the table (any rule; they are statements about n mod 8):
   T_n is even  <=>  n = 0 or 3 mod 4.   n = 1 mod 8  =>  T_n = 1 mod 4.   n = 5 mod 8  =>  T_n = 3 mod 4.
   verified for n < 4096: True
   Consequence for 3x+1: every index with Terras stopping time >= 4 (n = 3 mod 4) has an even T_n
   (value in Set_1); an index in Set_3 (n = 1 mod 4) always has an odd T_n.
================================================================================================
First-run law.  L1(n) = length of the first run of equal letters in the parity vector of n.
   3x+1:  L1(n) = v2(n) + v2(n+1) = v2(T_n) + 1         (T_n = n(n+1)/2: branch fixed points 0 and -1)
   3x-1:  L1(n) = v2(n) + v2(n-1) = v2(T_(n-1)) + 1     (fixed points 0 and +1)
   5x+1:  L1(n) = v2(n) + v2(3n+1) = v2(n(3n+1)/2) + 1  (fixed points 0 and -1/3; second pentagonal)
================================================================================================
   True   3x+1 vs T_n   (1 <= n <= 200000)
   True   3x-1 vs T_(n-1)   (1 <= n <= 200000)
   True   5x+1 vs n(3n+1)/2   (1 <= n <= 200000)
   False  control: 5x+1 vs T_n (should fail)   (1 <= n <= 200000)
   False  control: 3x-1 vs T_n (should fail)   (1 <= n <= 200000)
================================================================================================
Mutual information with the figurate family MATCHED to the rule (K = 16, n in Z/2^17):
   rule 3x+1 with T_n = n(n+1)/2      : I = 0.500000 bits
   rule 3x-1 with T_(n-1) = n(n-1)/2  : I = 0.500000 bits
   rule 5x+1 with n(3n+1)/2           : I = 0.266047 bits
   rule 5x-1 with n(3n-1)/2           : I = 0.324196 bits
   3x+-1: exactly 1/2 bit (theorem 6 of the README).  5x+-1: not 1/2.  The 3x value uses that the word
   '10' is already a dropping word (3 < 4), so every odd n outside the class k = 2 has first run >= 2.
